# Time Complexity (Big O Notation)

## Definition

**Time Complexity** measures how the number of operations performed by an algorithm grows as the input size (`n`) increases.

It does **not** measure the actual execution time in seconds.

---

# Why Do We Need Time Complexity?

Different computers have different hardware configurations, so the execution time of the same program may vary.

Time Complexity provides a standard way to compare the efficiency of algorithms regardless of the computer being used.

---

# Big O Notation

Big O Notation is used to represent the **worst-case time complexity** of an algorithm.

It describes how the running time grows as the input size increases.

---

# Common Time Complexities

## 1. O(1) – Constant Time

The execution time remains the same regardless of the input size.

### Example

```python
arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]
print(arr[2])
```

**Operations**

* 5 elements → 1 operation
* 100 elements → 1 operation
* 1000 elements → 1 operation

---

## 2. O(log n) – Logarithmic Time

The input size is reduced by half in each step.

### Example

* Binary Search

**Operations**

* 16 elements → 4 steps
* 32 elements → 5 steps
* 1024 elements → 10 steps

---

## 3. O(n) – Linear Time

The algorithm processes every element exactly once.

### Example

```python
arr = [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]

for num in arr:
    print(num)
```

**Operations**

* 10 elements → 10 operations
* 100 elements → 100 operations
* 1000 elements → 1000 operations

---

## 4. O(n log n) – Linearithmic Time

Usually seen in efficient sorting algorithms.

### Examples

* Merge Sort
* Heap Sort
* Average-case Quick Sort

---

## 5. O(n²) – Quadratic Time

Usually occurs when nested loops iterate over the same input.

### Example

```python
arr = [1, 2, 3]

for i in arr:
    for j in arr:
        print(i, j)
```

**Operations**

* 10 elements → 100 operations
* 100 elements → 10,000 operations
* 1000 elements → 1,000,000 operations

---

## 6. O(n³) – Cubic Time

Occurs when three nested loops are used.

### Example

```python
for i in range(n):
    for j in range(n):
        for k in range(n):
            print(i, j, k)
```

---

## 7. O(2ⁿ) – Exponential Time

Each recursive call generates two or more additional calls.

### Example

* Recursive Fibonacci

---

## 8. O(n!) – Factorial Time

The slowest commonly encountered time complexity.

### Examples

* Generating all permutations
* Traveling Salesman Problem (Brute Force)

---

# Time Complexity Order (Best to Worst)

| Complexity | Performance    |
| ---------- | -------------- |
| O(1)       | Excellent      |
| O(log n)   | Very Fast      |
| O(n)       | Good           |
| O(n log n) | Efficient      |
| O(n²)      | Slow           |
| O(n³)      | Very Slow      |
| O(2ⁿ)      | Extremely Slow |
| O(n!)      | Worst          |

---

# Rules for Calculating Time Complexity

### Rule 1: Ignore Constants

```python
for i in range(n):
    print(i)
```

Time Complexity = **O(n)**

Not **O(2n)** or **O(5n)**.

---

### Rule 2: Nested Loops Multiply

```python
for i in range(n):
    for j in range(n):
        print(i, j)
```

Time Complexity = **O(n²)**

---

### Rule 3: Consecutive Loops Add

```python
for i in range(n):
    print(i)

for j in range(n):
    print(j)
```

Time Complexity =

O(n) + O(n)

= **O(n)**

---

### Rule 4: Keep the Highest Order Term

Example

```
O(n² + n + 100)
```

Final Time Complexity

```
O(n²)
```

---

# Why Big O Matters

* Compares algorithms fairly.
* Helps choose efficient solutions.
* Predicts performance for large inputs.
* Essential for coding interviews.
* Used in software engineering and competitive programming.

---

# Interview Tips

* Always analyze the time complexity of your solution.
* Try to improve an O(n²) solution to O(n log n) or O(n) whenever possible.
* Binary Search is one of the most common O(log n) algorithms asked in interviews.

---

# Quick Revision

| Operation           | Time Complexity |
| ------------------- | --------------- |
| Access by Index     | O(1)            |
| Traverse Array      | O(n)            |
| Nested Loops        | O(n²)           |
| Binary Search       | O(log n)        |
| Merge Sort          | O(n log n)      |
| Recursive Fibonacci | O(2ⁿ)           |
| Permutations        | O(n!)           |

---

# Key Takeaways

* Time Complexity measures algorithm efficiency.
* Big O Notation represents the worst-case growth of an algorithm.
* Lower time complexity generally means better performance.
* O(1), O(log n), O(n), and O(n log n) are considered efficient for most applications.
* Understanding Time Complexity is essential before learning advanced Data Structures and Algorithms.
